# Keys whose signature the promotion boundary accepts.
#
# Format is git's allowed-signers format (see `man ssh-keygen`, ALLOWED SIGNERS):
#
#     <principal> <keytype> <base64 key>
#
# One line per key, no wildcards. This file is read by
# `daedalus.spine.promotion_approval` AS COMMITTED AT HEAD -- editing the
# working copy changes nothing, by design. Adding a key here is a reviewable
# commit, which is the point: it is the entire list of identities that can
# authorise a promotion.
#
# EMPTY OF KEYS ON PURPOSE. No signature can verify against this file as it
# stands, so every promotion refuses. That is the correct state until the
# owner adds their own public key: an allowed-signers file populated by an
# implementer would mean the implementer, not the owner, chose who may promote.
#
# The owner adds a key with, from the repository root:
#
#     ssh-keygen -Y find-principals ...   # to inspect an existing key, or
#     echo "owner@example ssh-ed25519 AAAA..." >> .agentenv/promotion_allowed_signers
#
# and commits it. See docs/GATE0_SEALED_OWNER_APPROVAL.md for the decision this
# file implements.
